"""Manual editorial guides with official sources; independent of offer snapshots."""
from html import escape

REVIEWED = '2026-10-02'
GUIDES = [
    dict(slug='how-to-choose-first-vps', category='HOSTING GUIDE',
         title='How to choose your first VPS without buying too much',
         summary='Use static hosting for a simple publication; consider a VPS when your app needs a running server. Then size it for the software, traffic and recovery plan.',
         sources=[('IONOS VPS features and purchase conditions', 'https://www.ionos.com/servers/vps'),
                  ('DigitalOcean Droplet billing', 'https://docs.digitalocean.com/products/droplets/details/pricing/'),
                  ('Hetzner Cloud server FAQ', 'https://docs.hetzner.com/cloud/servers/faq/')],
         body='''<h2>Start with the job, not the specification table</h2>
<p>Before browsing VPS plans, list what will run on the server: the app, database, background jobs and files. Add who will install updates and how you will restore it after a failure. If the site is only generated HTML and images, compare static hosting first; you may not need a server.</p>
<p>For a static publication, compare a managed static host with a VPS first. For an application, list its runtime, database, background jobs and files. For AI features, distinguish calling a remote API from running a model yourself. An application that sends requests to a hosted API does not need to fit the model weights into its own memory. A self-hosted model needs a separate hardware and licensing investigation; a provider's AI marketing label is not a capacity test.</p>
<h2>What size server do you need?</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Question before buying</th><th>Evidence to collect</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>CPU</td><td>Is the workload bursty, sustained or mostly waiting for another service?</td><td>Application measurements and the provider's shared or dedicated CPU description.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Memory</td><td>What must remain in memory together?</td><td>Runtime, database and background-job usage under representative requests.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storage</td><td>What grows over time and what can be rebuilt?</td><td>Database, uploads, logs, backups and update headroom.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Network</td><td>Where are readers, and what data leaves the server?</td><td>Region choices, transfer allowance and overage terms.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recovery</td><td>How will service return after a failed update?</td><td>Backup retention, restore procedure and a copy outside the running server.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Start with your software’s requirements and try a typical workload where the plan permits it. A server’s visitor capacity depends on the pages, caching and database work, so a RAM figure alone cannot tell you how much traffic it will handle.</p>
<h2>Choose a region around the reader and dependencies</h2>
<p>Put the application near the people or services it talks to most often. An English-language site does not automatically mean every visitor is in the United States. A content delivery network can serve static assets near readers, while dynamic requests still travel to the origin and database. Test from representative locations instead of assuming the closest map pin gives the fastest page. </p>
<h2>Include operations in the purchase</h2>
<p>Read the support scope. Access to a server console and assistance with the provider's infrastructure do not necessarily include repairing your application. Decide who installs updates, checks disk usage, reviews failed jobs and restores backups. A cheap unmanaged server can be a poor fit when nobody is available to maintain it. Conversely, paying for a managed bundle is unnecessary if your deployment does not use its extra services.</p>
<p>Keep a note of the app version, required environment-variable names, restore steps and backup location. Leave secret values out of that note. Do not store the only backup on the same disk as the running application. A snapshot is a dated recovery point, not proof that every recent database transaction is safely preserved. Check the provider's documentation and test an appropriate restore before relying on it.</p>
<h2>Check the exit before the entry</h2>
<p>Can the plan be resized, and does a resize require downtime? Can you export files and database data? What happens to extra disks, addresses and backups when the server is removed? Ask these questions while switching is still easy. Document what must be cancelled separately. Do not assume shutting down the operating system also stops billing: the provider's resource lifecycle and terms control charges.</p>
<p>Finish by comparing the whole commitment, rather than the first advertised month. Use our <a href="/compare/">verified offer comparison</a>, then read the <a href="/guides/how-to-read-vps-promo-terms/">promotion checklist</a> and <a href="/guides/vps-vs-managed-static-hosting/">deployment comparison</a>. Choose a plan that runs the app and gives you a workable recovery route. A discount badge cannot answer either question.</p>'''),
    dict(slug='how-to-read-vps-promo-terms', category='BUYING GUIDE',
         title='How to read VPS promotion terms before checkout',
         summary='Separate the introductory period, contract commitment and later charges before treating an offer as a saving.',
         sources=[('IONOS current VPS plan terms', 'https://www.ionos.com/servers/vps'),
                  ('DigitalOcean Droplet pricing documentation', 'https://docs.digitalocean.com/products/droplets/details/pricing/')],
         body='''<h2>Check three dates: promotion, contract and renewal</h2>
<p>A promotion may last three months while the contract lasts a year. Renewal terms decide what you pay after that agreement ends. Write down all three periods before comparing prices.</p>
<p>At this source review, the IONOS VPS page pairs its introductory offer with a three-month promotion and a one-year term. This is an example of why the promotion period and commitment must be read separately, not a promise about tomorrow's checkout. Our current offer pages retain the source retrieval time and purchase conditions. Reopen the official page before paying.</p>
<h2>Build a charge worksheet</h2>
<p>Copy the details from the actual checkout for your chosen plan and account region. Keep the currency, billing period and tax treatment beside each amount. Mark unknown amounts as unknown; an empty field is a reason to investigate, not permission to substitute the introductory price.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Charge</th><th>What to record</th><th>Where to confirm it</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Introductory service</td><td>Rate, eligible months and commitment</td><td>Plan terms and checkout</td></tr>
<tr><td>Later service</td><td>Rate after the promotion and renewal conditions</td><td>Contract summary</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extras</td><td>Backups, licenses, storage, addresses and support</td><td>Selected options and their billing terms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tax and currency</td><td>Whether tax is included, charged separately or determined at checkout</td><td>Final invoice or checkout summary</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exit</td><td>Cancellation notice and charges for resources retained</td><td>Cancellation documentation and agreement</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Calculate the cost over the same period</h2>
<p>For a fixed-term comparison, use: total commitment cost = introductory rate × introductory months + later rate × remaining months + mandatory fees + selected extras + applicable tax. Every input must come from the relevant provider's checkout or agreement. If later charges or tax are unknown, do not claim a total or savings percentage. Usage-based services require an additional workload estimate and a separately identified rate schedule.</p>
<p>The cheapest advertised month can therefore differ from the cheapest plan over your actual project lifetime. Compare the same period and the same required features. A prepaid annual commitment and a flexible server are not interchangeable just because both display a monthly amount. Use the amount you will actually pay, not the crossed-out reference price.</p>
<h2>Read network language carefully</h2>
<p>Traffic allowance describes a quantity of transferred data; bandwidth describes a rate. Neither, on its own, tells you the application's response time. When a page says unlimited traffic, check the terms that accompany that exact product. Ask about acceptable use, throttling, excluded destinations and any plan-specific restrictions. Do not import conditions from a different provider or region.</p>
<h2>Check eligibility and cancellation before ordering</h2>
<p>A promotion may depend on the account, location, payment term or product variation. Follow the official purchase path and read the final summary. Do not buy through an unofficial reseller to obtain an unverified coupon. If a trial or refund policy is relevant to your decision, check what it covers and whether add-ons have different conditions. Cancellation, refund and deletion are separate actions; none should be assumed to imply the others.</p>
<p>Record the agreement you accepted privately, with an order reference, and set your own review reminder before any relevant deadline. Do not post invoices, bank details or account identifiers in a public issue. After cancellation, check whether disks, snapshots or other separately billed resources remain. The provider's billing documentation is the authority for what continues to incur charges.</p>
<p>Return to <a href="/compare/">the verified comparison</a> for current records, <a href="/guides/how-to-choose-first-vps/">the first VPS guide</a> for workload fit and <a href="/guides/vps-vs-managed-static-hosting/">the hosting choice guide</a> before assuming you need a server at all.</p>'''),
    dict(slug='vps-vs-managed-static-hosting', category='DEPLOYMENT GUIDE',
         title='VPS or managed static hosting for your next website?',
         summary='For generated HTML and images, start with static hosting. A VPS is worth considering when you need to run and maintain server software.',
         sources=[('Cloudflare Pages product documentation', 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/'),
                  ('IONOS VPS features', 'https://www.ionos.com/servers/vps'),
                  ('Hugging Face managed inference documentation', 'https://huggingface.co/docs/inference-endpoints/index')],
         body='''<h2>Begin with what runs after a visitor arrives</h2>
<p>If your deployment produces HTML, CSS and images, managed static hosting is a sensible option to investigate first. If the website needs a persistent process, a database or background workers, describe those components before selecting hosting. Adding an AI chat feature does not automatically turn the project into a GPU server: it may call an external model API instead.</p>
<p>Cloudflare documents Pages as a website deployment platform and separately documents its Functions capability. That means a static project and a project using server-side functions can have different limits and operating costs. Do not treat a provider's free entry point as a guarantee that every future feature remains free. Read the documentation for the features you actually plan to enable.</p>
<h2>Compare responsibility as well as features</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Useful question</th><th>Responsibility to plan</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Managed static hosting</td><td>Can the site be generated before deployment?</td><td>Build reproducibility, custom domain, redirects and any function usage.</td></tr>
<tr><td>VPS</td><td>Do you need control over persistent software?</td><td>Operating system updates, application security, monitoring and recovery.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Managed inference endpoint</td><td>Do you need to serve a model through an endpoint?</td><td>Model choice, hardware configuration, scaling and inference billing.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>With a VPS, you maintain the operating system and running app. A managed platform handles some of that work but sets limits on builds, functions and other features. Compare the maintenance you can take on with the features your site needs.</p>
<h2>Keep browser code and secrets apart</h2>
<p>Anything delivered in a static page can be inspected by visitors. Keep private API credentials out of generated HTML, public repositories and downloadable JavaScript. If a feature requires a secret, use an appropriate server-side component and its documented secret management. Limit requests and spending there, rather than relying only on a button being hidden in the browser.</p>
<p>A public chat or generation endpoint needs an abuse plan. Consider repeated requests, large inputs and retries. A monthly editor subscription is not payment for calls made by website visitors. Set a usage budget before making the feature public, and make failure states understandable. A page should be able to explain a temporary limit without repeatedly triggering another paid request.</p>
<h2>Plan a move before it becomes urgent</h2>
<p>Keep the source repository, build command, environment requirements and domain settings documented. For a static site, check that a fresh build produces the same routes and canonical domain. For an application, add a database export and restore procedure. A successful deploy alone does not prove you can recover from a lost server or move away from a platform.</p>
<p>Compare renewal costs and the recurring work needed to operate the site. Include the features you actually require instead of assuming every optional add-on belongs in the budget. Use the <a href="/guides/how-to-choose-first-vps/">VPS checklist</a>, <a href="/guides/how-to-read-vps-promo-terms/">promotion terms guide</a> and <a href="/compare/">current hosting records</a> together. The right outcome may be to postpone buying a VPS until a real application requirement appears.</p>'''),
    dict(slug='evaluate-new-ai-model', category='AI BUYING GUIDE', ai=True,
         title='A new AI model launched. Should your project switch?',
         summary='Switch only if a small test on your own tasks shows a useful improvement. Check access, billing and rollback before changing production.',
         sources=[('Gemini API release notes', 'https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/changelog'),
                  ('Anthropic official announcements', 'https://www.anthropic.com/news'),
                  ('Hugging Face endpoint documentation', 'https://huggingface.co/docs/inference-endpoints/index')],
         body='''<h2>What would the new model improve?</h2>
<p>A model announcement is a reason to investigate, not a reason to replace working software immediately. Identify the part of your application that could benefit: code review, document extraction, customer questions or another specific task. If you cannot describe what would improve, keep the current setup while you gather evidence.</p>
<p>Separate vendor statements from your own results. Release notes establish what the vendor announced, when it happened and which interface changed. They do not demonstrate the effect on your users. A benchmark headline cannot tell you whether the model follows your output format, handles your edge cases or fits your spending limit.</p>
<h2>Check the model name and access</h2>
<p>Write down the provider, model identifier, interface and availability status. A consumer subscription, an API model and a downloadable model are different products. Check whether the model is generally available, in preview or restricted to existing accounts. Read migration and retirement notices as well as launch posts. Replacing a model string is insufficient when tool arguments or response formats also change.</p>
<h2>Prepare a small, representative evaluation set</h2>
<p>Use tasks for which you can judge the expected result. Include ordinary examples, ambiguous instructions, missing information and inputs the system should reject. Remove private customer data and production credentials. Record success criteria before comparing outputs, so a persuasive answer does not move the goalposts afterward.</p>
<p>Keep the application settings and instructions documented. Measure the complete workflow, including validation, retries and human review. An answer that looks better but breaks the downstream parser is not a successful migration. </p>
<h2>Compare the whole request bill</h2>
<p>Use the provider's current rates for the exact model and feature. Separate input, output, cached usage, tools and any separately charged service. Keep representative usage counts from your own test next to their source. A shorter advertised response does not prove a lower application bill when retries increase.</p>
<p>Track the resulting cost per completed task rather than the price label alone. If you have no measured usage yet, leave the estimate open and try a small task with a spending cap. Do not count temporary credits as permanent savings. Decide how the application behaves when its spending limit is reached.</p>
<h2>Keep a way back to the old model</h2>
<p>Keep the old configuration available, test outside production and verify logs without storing unnecessary private inputs. Check output compatibility before exposing the change to everyone. A rollback should restore both the model and any associated prompt or parser change. Be cautious about giving a new model broader tool permissions merely because the launch calls it agentic.</p>
<p>Use <a href="/ai-news/">our AI update desk</a> to find dated official changes, <a href="/guides/ai-subscription-vs-api/">the subscription versus API guide</a> to clarify billing and <a href="/ai-tools/ai-app-budget/">the application budget guide</a> to connect model costs to hosting. Switch when a small comparison shows a benefit for your project; a release alone does not settle that decision.</p>'''),
    dict(slug='ai-subscription-vs-api', category='AI COST GUIDE', ai=True,
         title='AI subscriptions, APIs and hosting are separate purchases',
         summary='Trace who is using the model and where the request is billed before adding another subscription.',
         sources=[('Cursor plans', 'https://cursor.com/pricing'),
                  ('GitHub Copilot plans', 'https://github.com/features/copilot/plans'),
                  ('Gemini API documentation', 'https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs')],
         body='''<h2>Follow the request</h2>
<p>Start with who sends the request. An editor assistant helps you build software. A visitor using your deployed application may trigger a request from your server to an API. The server hosting that application is another service. Before purchasing, identify which account pays for each action instead of assuming a single AI subscription covers the entire project.</p>
<p>Cursor and GitHub Copilot publish their own plan conditions. A supported model inside one tool does not establish API entitlement with the model provider. Likewise, an API key does not automatically include an editor's paid features. Check the agreement for the exact product. This guide does not assert that any subscription grants unlimited model access.</p>
<h2>Make a purchase map</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Purchase</th><th>Who uses it?</th><th>What to verify</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Development assistant</td><td>You while building and reviewing</td><td>Included usage, editor compatibility and extra charges.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Model API</td><td>Your application or automation</td><td>Supported model, usage rates, request limits and data handling.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hosting</td><td>The running website</td><td>Runtime, traffic, storage, recovery and renewal terms.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Do you need both subscriptions?</h2>
<p>Give each subscription a job. If two assistants both serve the same editing task, evaluate whether either has a distinct benefit you actually use. Test within their permitted entry plans when available. Do not buy a bundle simply because a model name is fashionable or because a promotion expires soon. Availability and plan limits can change, so confirm them on the official page at checkout.</p>
<h2>Separate fixed and variable spending</h2>
<p>Subscriptions may have recurring fees as well as variable usage. APIs can include different billing categories, and hosting may add storage or transfer charges. Keep these separate in your budget. If a visitor can trigger model calls, a predictable development subscription does not make your deployed feature's bill predictable.</p>
<p>Watch repeated requests and failed responses. Validate inputs before invoking a paid service, avoid unnecessary retries and decide where usage limits are enforced. Store credentials in a suitable server-side secret mechanism. Never put a private API key into a static page to make integration easier.</p>
<h2>Consider the end of the promotion</h2>
<p>Credits and temporary allowances are useful for evaluation, but they are not ongoing operating income. Decide what the application will cost or stop doing after they end. Record the source and expiry conditions for any credit you rely on. If those details are unknown, do not publish a savings claim or promise users permanent free access.</p>
<p>Connect this decision to the <a href="/ai-tools/ai-app-budget/">AI application budget</a>, <a href="/guides/evaluate-new-ai-model/">model-switch checklist</a> and <a href="/guides/vps-vs-managed-static-hosting/">hosting comparison</a>. Give each purchase a job and a line in the budget. Cancel overlapping tools you do not use.</p>'''),
]

def publish_guides(page, cfg):
    ai = cfg.get('ai_tools_enabled') == 'true'
    visible = [g for g in GUIDES if ai or not g.get('ai')]
    def publish(path, title, description, body):
        page(path, title, description, body, lastmod='2026-10-07')
    cards = ''.join(f'<article class="card"><p class="eyebrow">{escape(g["category"])}</p><h3><a href="/guides/{g["slug"]}/">{escape(g["title"])}</a></h3><p>{escape(g["summary"])}</p><a class="arrow" href="/guides/{g["slug"]}/">Read the guide →</a></article>' for g in visible)
    publish('/guides/', 'Hosting and AI buying guides | '+cfg['brand'], 'Practical decisions about VPS terms, deployment and AI spending.',
            '<section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">PRACTICAL GUIDES</p><h1>Buy for the project.<br><span>Read beyond the headline.</span></h1><p class="lede">Hosting terms, deployment choices and AI costs explained around the work you want to finish.</p></section><section class="section"><div class="grid">'+cards+'</div></section><p class="notice">These guides explain buying decisions using official documentation. They are not performance tests.</p>')
    providers = ''.join(f'<li><a href="/providers/{escape(p["id"])}/">{escape(p["name"])} current source status</a></li>' for p in cfg['providers'])
    for g in visible:
        sources = ''.join(f'<li><a href="{escape(url)}" rel="noopener">{escape(label)}</a></li>' for label, url in g['sources'])
        body = f'<section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">{escape(g["category"])}</p><h1>{escape(g["title"])}</h1><p class="lede">{escape(g["summary"])}</p></section><article class="prose">{g["body"]}<h2>Current provider records</h2><ul>{providers}</ul><h2>Sources</h2><ul>{sources}</ul><p>Source review date: {REVIEWED}. Based on the sources above, not a product performance test. Source links are non-affiliate links; check current terms before buying.</p><p><a href="/guides/">← All guides</a> · <a href="/methodology/">How we verify offers</a></p></article>'
        publish('/guides/'+g['slug']+'/',g['title'],g['summary'],body)
    if not ai:
        return
    publish('/ai-news/', 'AI updates for developers | '+cfg['brand'], 'Dated official model updates interpreted for website builders.',
            '<section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">AI UPDATE DESK</p><h1>What changed.<br><span>What it means for your project.</span></h1><p class="lede">Model releases and migration notices, with official evidence and practical checks before switching.</p></section><section class="section"><article class="card"><p class="eyebrow">SOURCE REVIEW: '+REVIEWED+'</p><h2><a href="/ai-news/gemini-flash-migration-check/">Gemini Flash: model access and migration checks</a></h2><p>Read Google\'s dated release and access notices before selecting a model for a new application.</p><a class="arrow" href="/ai-news/gemini-flash-migration-check/">Read the update →</a></article></section><article class="prose"><h2>Use an update to answer a decision</h2><p><a href="/guides/evaluate-new-ai-model/">Should your project switch models?</a> · <a href="/guides/ai-subscription-vs-api/">Which account pays for the request?</a> · <a href="/ai-tools/">Browse tool buying guides</a></p><h2>Coverage and sources</h2><p>This is a manually reviewed selection, not a complete or automatically refreshed news feed. We distinguish announcement dates, our review date and our editorial conclusions. We do not copy press releases or present vendor benchmarks as our own tests.</p><ul><li><a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/changelog" rel="noopener">Google Gemini API release notes</a></li><li><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news" rel="noopener">Anthropic official newsroom</a></li></ul></article>')
    publish('/ai-news/gemini-flash-migration-check/', 'Gemini Flash: check access and migration before switching', 'What Google\'s September notices mean for a new Gemini API project.',
            '''<section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">AI UPDATE / GOOGLE</p><h1>Check access before you switch Gemini models.</h1><p class="lede">Google's dated API notices are useful for choosing a model identifier. They do not replace testing your application's behavior.</p></section><article class="prose"><h2>What the official record says</h2><p>Google's release notes dated September 2, 2026 announce general availability of Gemini 3.8 Flash, with the identifier <code>gemini-3.8-flash</code>. The September 18 notice says access to Gemini 2.5 models is limited to users who previously used them; it also says those models are not deprecated and recommends newer models for new projects. The notices describe access, not a comparison of model performance.</p><p><a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/changelog" rel="noopener">Read the official dated release notes</a> and <a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models" rel="noopener">current model documentation</a>. Source review: 2026-10-02. This article covers those specific notices and does not claim to list every subsequent announcement.</p><h2>Why this matters to a website builder</h2><p>If a tutorial points to an older model, do not assume your new project can access it. Check the exact model identifier against current documentation and your account's permitted access. An access limitation is different from retirement: changing the error handling and replacing the entire application are different responses. This distinction can prevent unnecessary rewrites.</p><h2>Our suggested migration check</h2><p>Keep the existing configuration recorded and work in a test environment. Confirm the model accepts your input format, returns the output your parser expects and supports any tools you use. Run representative requests, including failure cases. Compare the complete task rather than a single impressive answer. Use the test results to decide whether switching helps your app.</p><p>Review the current pricing for the exact model and features before enabling public usage. Set an application spending limit and decide what happens when requests fail or retry. Do not assume an editor subscription includes API calls from visitors, or that the server hosting the website runs the remote model itself.</p><h2>Before making it public</h2><p>Check both success and rejection paths, keep private API keys out of browser code and prepare a rollback. Preserve only the logs necessary to investigate failures; do not turn testing into a new collection of private user prompts. Check the official notes again when you deploy, because this page is a dated editorial review rather than live account information.</p><p>Continue with <a href="/guides/evaluate-new-ai-model/">the model evaluation checklist</a>, <a href="/guides/ai-subscription-vs-api/">subscription versus API billing</a> and <a href="/ai-tools/ai-app-budget/">the application budget guide</a>.</p><p>Source links are non-affiliate links. This is a documentation review, not a model test. <a href="/ai-news/">← AI update desk</a></p></article>''')
